#378 (F14) — resolved-thread + head-freshness awareness: the CONSUMPTION half.

  HUMAN-APPLY-ONLY. Do NOT merge this via the autonomous merge driver.

engine/src/roles/reviewer.ts and engine/src/roles/merge-driver.ts are listed in
PROTECTED_SUFFIXES (engine/src/guard/guard.ts) and in docs/security.md's
"Human-merge-only paths": a worker must not edit the review/merge gate it is
itself gated by. So issue #378's ACs #2-#4 ship as this patch artifact instead
of as a branch, per the issue's own "Human-owned remainder" section.

DEPENDS ON: the forge.ts data-plumbing PR for #378 (AC #1), which must be merged
first. This patch consumes `ReviewThreadSpan` / `PRReviewData.threads` /
`findingDigest`, none of which exist before it.

APPLY:
    git apply -p1 docs/patches/378-resolved-thread-head-freshness.patch
    (cd engine && npm run typecheck && npm run lint && npm test)

MOTIVATING CASE (documentation, no new live run required — issue #378 step 6):
PR #366, dogfood run 2026-07-24. The same config-YAML finding was raised FIVE
times across re-reviews — twice against a stale head — after it had been
human-adjudicated, thread-resolved, and its remedy merged elsewhere (PR #367).
Each re-flag re-entered the FIXABLE gate and consumed a fix-round evaluation,
contributing to the lane hitting prFixCap and gatedReentryCap.

WHAT IT DOES:
  - reviewer.ts `adjudicatedDuplicateThreads`: an unresolved thread carrying the
    SAME finding (`findingDigest`) at the SAME span (path + line) as an
    already-RESOLVED thread whose code has not moved since (GitHub's own
    `isOutdated` is false) is an adjudicated re-raise, and is removed from
    `deriveBlockingSignal`'s count so it never routes the lane into another fix
    round. Matching is NOT by thread id — a re-raise always arrives as a new
    thread id, which is exactly why "is this id resolved?" answered no five
    times running on PR #366.
  - reviewer.ts `staleHeadReviewCount`: makes the already-existing exclusion of
    non-current-head reviews COUNTABLE. This adds no new filtering — both halves
    of the gate already bind to the current head (`freshHeadReviewCount` for
    approval, `changesRequestedOnHead` for blocking) — it makes the exclusion
    visible in the audit trail and pins it with regression tests, instead of
    leaving "advisory only" as an emergent consequence of two separate filters.
  - merge-driver.ts: the FIXABLE outcome's reason now reports the count actually
    acted on plus `adjudicatedDuplicates=` and `staleHeadReviews=`. A filter that
    silently shrinks gate② input would be precisely the kind of invisible
    weakening this repo's doctrine refuses.

WHY THE KEY IS (finding, span) AND NOT SPAN ALONE:
The engine-agent review of PR #445 found a real defect in the first revision of
this patch, which keyed adjudication on file:line only. Two unrelated findings
can share a line — "missing required key `foo`" adjudicated in round 1, "wrong
indentation" raised fresh in round 2 on that same still-current line — and a
span-only key would treat the second, never-adjudicated finding as a duplicate
and silently drop it from gate② input. That is a gate weakening, not a saved fix
round. The key now includes `findingDigest`, and the collision case is a test.
`findingDigest` is a last-resort text comparison (a review bot emits prose, not
a typed event with a rule id), so per this repo's authoritative-signals doctrine
it is kept as narrow as possible — whitespace-run normalization only, no case
folding, no fuzzy matching — and it favours the FALSE-NEGATIVE direction: a
reworded re-raise goes unrecognized and keeps blocking (one more fix round, the
bounded waste #378 set out to reduce), rather than two distinct findings
collapsing into one (a suppressed finding).

WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT WEAKEN (pinned by the tests in this patch):
  - a DIFFERENT finding on an already-adjudicated span still blocks;
  - the SAME finding at a different span still blocks;
  - a thread with no readable finding body is unkeyable and never filtered;
  - an unresolved thread with no prior adjudication on its span still blocks;
  - a resolved thread whose code CHANGED after resolution reads as outdated, does
    not adjudicate anything, and its re-raise still blocks;
  - an unresolved thread that is itself outdated is never filtered;
  - a file-level thread (no keyable span) is never filtered;
  - a standing CHANGES_REQUESTED still blocks even when every thread is filtered;
  - absent thread data (any pre-#378 fixture or forge fake) filters nothing at
    all — byte-for-byte the pre-#378 gate.

REPRODUCING THE PRE-SUBMISSION VERIFICATION (do not take the numbers on trust —
the engine-agent review of PR #445 correctly noted that a deleted dry-run tree
is not independently checkable). The producing worker is forbidden from writing
the real paths, so it type-checked, linted and RAN this exact content as a
sibling module tree. To reproduce that on the PR branch without applying
anything to the protected files:

    mkdir -p engine/src/roles378
    cp engine/src/roles/{reviewer,merge-driver,reviewer.test,merge-driver.test}.ts \
       engine/src/roles378/
    sed 's|engine/src/roles/|engine/src/roles378/|g' \
       docs/patches/378-resolved-thread-head-freshness.patch > /tmp/378re.patch
    git apply -p1 /tmp/378re.patch
    (cd engine && npm run typecheck && npx biome ci src/roles378 \
       && node --import tsx --test src/roles378/reviewer.test.ts \
                                  src/roles378/merge-driver.test.ts)
    rm -rf engine/src/roles378

Expected: 273 tests pass, 0 fail; typecheck and lint clean. `git apply --check
-p1` against the real paths is likewise clean. Applying the patch for real is
still the human step.

